// Conquord subject: src/fiat2/eval/Kafka_Cluster.v
//
// WHERE COZY SHOULD WIN, and the reason this spec is here.  `onePartition`
// asks about a COMPOSITE key -- Kafka built a map on
// `new TopicPartition(topic, partition)` by hand -- and Conquord has no
// composite-key index, so it probes on topic and filters the bucket
// (one_partition_probes_then_filters).  Cozy builds maps keyed on tuples,
// so it should find the map Kafka wrote and we do not.
//
// FOUND BY RUNNING IT: written with `the [...]`, as Kafka's accessor returns
// one row, Cozy REJECTS the spec -- "`the` is illegal since its argument may
// not be singleton" -- until the composite uniqueness is declared as an
// invariant.  That is a capability Conquord does not have: `DUnique` names one
// attribute, so a two-attribute key cannot be declared at all.  The spec below
// therefore returns the filtered collection, which is the same information the
// subject is given (Kafka_Cluster declares nothing), and cluster_declared.ds
// is the same spec WITH the composite invariant, to see what that buys.
//
// Encoding decisions:
//   * `leader` is a nullable Node in Java and TOption TInt in the subject.
//     Cozy has no option type, so an absent leader is -1 and the
//     availability read asks for leader != -1.
//   * Cluster is immutable (withPartitions builds a new one), so there is
//     no op here either.
Cluster:

    type Partition = { topic : String, partition : Int, leader : Int }

    state partitions : Bag<Partition>

    query forTopic(t : String)
        [p | p <- partitions, p.topic == t]

    query forNode(n : Int)
        [p | p <- partitions, p.leader == n]

    query onePartition(t : String, part : Int)
        [p | p <- partitions, p.topic == t, p.partition == part]

    query available(t : String)
        [p | p <- partitions, p.topic == t, p.leader != -1]
